This dataset contains Controlling Blood Pressure (CBP) measure prediction results for the WellMed population. The CBP measure is defined as the percentage of members aged 18–85 who have been diagnosed with hypertension (HTN) and have adequately controlled blood pressure during the measurement year. Adequate control is defined as having a representative systolic blood pressure of less than 140 mm Hg and a representative diastolic blood pressure of less than 90 mm Hg.

The representative blood pressure reading is the most recent reading taken during the measurement year on or after the member’s second hypertension diagnosis. If multiple readings are taken or recorded on the same date, the lowest systolic and diastolic readings are used. Members without a recorded blood pressure reading during the measurement year are considered not controlled.

The prediction results help identify members at risk of not meeting the CBP measure and support targeted outreach and intervention efforts to improve blood pressure control.
